    "user_request_original": "Perform a numerical optimization of a 6-layer all-dielectric polarizing beam splitter on a fused silica substrate. Incident medium: Air. Substrate: Fused Silica. Incidence angle: 45 degrees. Wavelength range: 500-650 nm. Materials: TiO2 and SiO2 for the coating layers. Stack sequence: Air | TiO2 | SiO2 | TiO2 | SiO2 | TiO2 | SiO2 | Fused Silica. Design variables: Thicknesses of all 6 coating layers, bounded between 25 nm and 220 nm. Objective: Maximize a composite score that rewards Mean TE Reflectance (target >= 90%) and Mean TM Transmittance (target >= 85%), while penalizing total physical thickness. After finding the optimal thicknesses, perform a robustness analysis by simulating the response with independent normally distributed layer-thickness errors (sigma = 1.5% of nominal thickness) and a common incidence-angle offset uniformly distributed between -1 and +1 degree. Report the optimal thicknesses, the mean TE reflectance, mean TM transmittance, total thickness, and the statistical distribution of performance under uncertainty. Canonical user controls: incidence angles 45 degrees. polarization s, p. wavelength intervals 500-650 nm. soft objectives: high TE reflectance (mean >= 90%); high TM transmittance (mean >= 85%); fewer layers; lower total thickness. robustness conditions: layer thicknesses bounded between 25 nm and 220 nm; total layer counts restricted to 6, 10, or 14.",
